WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.340
This is a Second Thought video in collaboration with Better Ideas.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.400
Make sure to watch his video after this.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.520
In a previous episode we looked at what would happen to your body

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.240
if you stopped drinking soda.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.640
The list was pretty impressive,

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.100
and the most common question in the comment was

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.480
"What about caffeine?"

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.300
So, in this video we'll be answering just that

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.060
So, let's get started!

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.620
We all know that caffeine is responsible for that little energy boost you get when you drink a cup of coffee

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.380
But, what exactly is it?

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.700
Caffeine is a natural stimulant found most commonly in coffee, tea and cocoa plants

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.920
The first brewed tea has been traced back as far as 27.000 BCE

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.080
And caffeine is currently the most widely used

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.520
psychoactive drug in the world,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.580
with 80% of the world's population consuming a caffeinated product everyday

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.460
and upwards of 90% of adults in North America.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.760
Caffeine's main effect is on your brain.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.500
It blocks the production of adenosine,  the chemical responsible for making you feel tired.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.420
It also stimulates the production of dopamine and adrenaline,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.240
giving you that rush you crave before work or school.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.220
And there's the main problem with caffeine:

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.100
it's incredibly addictive.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.720
How many of you know someone who says "Don't talk to me before I have my coffee"?

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.320
Or "I can't function in the morning without my Starbucks".

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.760
Probably quite a few.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.520
As annoying as that is, people who say things like that probably actually mean it.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.240
Their body is so accustomed to a caffeine hit at the same time each morning

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.260
that if they don't get it they start to experience withdrawal

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:27.740
The symptoms of withdrawal usually begin 12 to 20 hours from your last caffeine fix,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.540
peaking two days later and generally lasting about a week

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.420
if you don't cave and have a soda or a cup of coffee to make it go away.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.820
Withdrawal symptoms include mild to severe headaches,

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:39.480
depending on how much caffeine you usually consume.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.460
Generally, your headaches will be very mild

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.095
if your body is used to less than 500 milligrams of caffeine per day.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.080
That's about five cups of coffee,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.140
which seems like a lot to many of us

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.080
but there are people who drink all that coffee or even more.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.760
And it's not just coffee that counts towards that 500mg figure.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.595
If you have a soda with lunch and tea in the afternoon

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.020
they'll contribute to your caffeine dependency too

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.360
If you're trying to cut off caffeine entirely,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.140
the list of withdrawal symptoms is pretty intimidating

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.080
First and most obviously, caffeine is a stimulant

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.640
so you'll loose the little energy kick you had relied on to get through the day.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.000
You'll also miss out on the psychological mood and concentration boost

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.300
your favorite caffeinated drink provides

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.280
One of the drawbacks of caffeine for people with anxiety issues

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.340
is that the increased adrenaline production can actually trigger symptoms

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.240
that feel like a panic attack.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.745
If you're trying to quit caffeine to avoid this issue,

00:02:28.745 --> 00:02:30.740
well, we've got some bad news for you.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.580
Caffeine withdrawal can actually cause depression and anxiety too

00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:36.735
According to one study by Johns Hopkins

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.360
about 13% of people suffer significant impairment while trying to quit.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.520
You'll also feel in increasingly irritable

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.820
every little thing will get on your nerves

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.460
You'll probably suffer from constipation for a few days

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:52.580
since caffeine stimulates bowel activity and your body has become accustomed to tit

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.300
Strangely enough you might have to deal with insomnia for a week or so

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.000
Typically, we think consuming caffeine as a culprit behind insomnia,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.260
and while that often is the case, your body will protest the lack of caffeine too;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.940
and you'll be specially aware of it when you're lying in bed trying to sleep.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.320
This all sounds pretty unpleasant, but the health benefits are probably worth it.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.200
After the painful withdrawal period you'll sleep better and feel more rested during the day.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.440
You'll likely won't feel  the need for an afternoon pick me up to make it through the rest of the day.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.640
Caffeine has been linked more and more closely with panic attacks and anxiety disorders - the most common form of mental illness in the United States

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.320
Cutting back on caffeine could very likely lessen their severity.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.300
Your headaches should disappear - at least those you associated with lack of coffee or caffeinated beverages.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:44.360
Your teeth will thank you as well. Caffeinated drinks, specially dark sodas and coffee are notorious for staining the enamel of your teeth,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.120
giving you a less charming yellower smile.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.900
Cutting these drinks from your daily routine will help keep your teeth healthy and bright.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.100
Finally,  you'll suffer less from heartburn .

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.680
Over 20% of all Americans report that the experience heartburn at least once a week.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.980
And 7% experience it daily

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:06.960
Coffee is a particularly bad trigger for people who suffer from heartburn, so reducing your coffee intake will hell prevent painful episodes

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.240
All that being said, caffeine isn't all bad.

